y have both a magnitude and direction. This\nis especially useful when thinking about explaining how the wind will blow.\nAny vector can be broken down into its components from which the magnitude\n(e.g., speed of the wind) and direction can be calculated.","position":{"start":{"line":29,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"key":"ZYrq2fFDdZ"}],"key":"oUXO4rICVg"}],"key":"IMhBs884kT"}],"key":"FaiL9PexNM"}],"key":"bgOdoH019w"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Practice Tasks - Contour Plotting","url":"/contour-plot-practice-tasks","group":"Introduction to Weather Technology using MetPy"},"next":{"title":"Hydrostatic Balance","url":"/hydrostatic-balance","group":"Introduction to Weather Technology using MetPy"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3001"}